{"text":"Closest foundations: [TiSASRec](https://jiachengli1995.github.io/files/wsdm20.pdf) learns attention using item positions and pairwise time intervals; its temporal mechanism is relevant, although its causal backbone should not replace BERT4Rec. [MEANTIME](https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.08273) provides a closer architectural precedent: multiple temporal embeddings and differentiated attention mechanisms within one jointly trained network. [TemProxRec (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.09784) explicitly combines absolute and relative temporal/positional embeddings, plus contrastive learning for interactions occurring near each other across users.

Recent directions extend beyond uniform recency decay. [TiSRec (RecSys 2025)](https://doi.org/10.1145/3705328.3759327) learns time-gap-based local preference blocks and models dependencies within and across blocks. [TSAPE (WWW 2026)](https://doi.org/10.1145/3774904.3792333) proposes adaptive positional encoding informed by interaction intervals. These support treating elapsed time and sequence distance as distinct signals. However, interpreting Amazon interaction gaps as dwell time or evidence of fulfilled needs would be unsupported.

A compatible experimental hypothesis: retain bidirectional masked-item BERT4Rec and add jointly learned, per-head relative-position and bucketed time-gap attention terms. Heads could learn different temporal sensitivities, preserving distant relevant interactions while emphasizing recent intent where useful; this separation is a hypothesis, not an established guarantee. Use only observed-history timestamps, with a consistent unknown-time convention for the appended prediction mask—never the held-out target timestamp. Keep one network/checkpoint per category and compare position-only, time-only, and combined variants using the fixed macro validation HR@10."}
